        "geoBonBiome": [
          "Fresh water rivers"
        ],
        "geology": "Geologically, ORACLE is located on the Brie plateau (137, 5 m of altitude). The main aquifers are: 1) the aquifer of the Brie limestone (or Oligocene aquifer), 2) the aquifer of the Champigny limestone (or upper Eocene aquifer). Other aquifers exist deeper but they may not interfere in the hydrology of the watershed. Rivers are incised in this Brie tableland and many springs, corresponding to the Brie and to the Champigny aquifers, can be seen along hill slopes.\r\n",
        "hydrology": "Oriented east- west , the layouts of the Grand and Petit Morin present a very undulating course of 120 km for the Grand Morin (divides into two arms) and 86 km for the Petit Morin. The mean annual flows are 3,4 m3/s and  7,6 m3/s for the Petit and Grand Morin respectively. The Orgeval sub-catchement, 12 km oriented north-south,  presents a mean annual flow about 0.6 m3/s.",
        "soils": "ORACLE is characterized by an hydromorphic silty loess soil. The tertiary substratum is covered by a quaternary loess deposit (up to 10 m thick). The upper layer is constituted of loess silt and the lower one is enriched in clay and sand.  This silty glossic leached brown soil is characterized by low permeability resulting in temporary waterlogged soil in winter, and the presence of a shallow aquifer.  Soils are naturally poorly drained.  Subsurface drainage has been installed starting in the 1960s. ",
        "vegetation": "The Morin basins are primarily agricultural (80% agricultural, 15% forester, 5% urban), and consistent with field crop regions."
      },
